  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nUpper level ridging is expected to remain across the region through \nmost of the week, with the main upper level jet remaining just to \nthe north across the Dakotas. Even though ridging is expected to \npersist, several shortwaves are expected to track through the flow, \npotentially bringing rain chances throughout the week. Aiding these \nprecipitation chances is the suggestion of a tongue of deeper \nmoisture aloft tracking into the region. With this expecting push of \nmoisture, ensemble guidance is actually fairly optimistic on \nmeasureable rainfall across the region, generally bringing at least \na 40 percent chance of light rain Monday onward. However, this is \ngenerally expected to remain light, as ensembles still show lower \nchance on amounts exceeding one tenth of an inch, which generally \nonly shows a 20 to 30 percent chance, with chances increasing later \nin the week. While the precipitation potential certainly seems to be \ntrending up, will still keep an eye on this, as it may be fairly \nlight regardless of chances and the potential push of moisture. \n\nWhile precipitation remains uncertain, there is a stronger signal \nregarding temperature trends next week. Expecting that the heat this \nweekend will last into at least Monday, where again highs may climb \ninto the mid to upper 90s, with a few locations across southwest \nNebraska potentially breaking 100. With these hot temperatures \npersisting, may still see some additional elevated to near critical \nfire weather concerns across western Nebraska. Keeping this threat \nsomewhat limited is an expected push of better humidity during the \nafternoon hours, though conditions may still get gusty Monday and \nTuesday. However, from there, expect a cooling trend to begin, with \ncooling beginning in earnest by mid week. In fact, expecting highs \nto reach the 80s by Wednesday, with potential for highs in the 70s \nto close out the week. Still a bit of uncertainty in this happening, \nbut the high temperature forecast is trending cooler. For reference, \ntypical highs for mid August are around 87 to 89 degrees. These \nforecast highs in the 70s would be nearly 15 degrees cooler than \naverage and generally in the 10th percentile for our local \nclimatology, so not completely unfeasible but rather unusual. While \nthe precise temperatures are still uncertain, do feel that \nconfidence in a pattern shift is relatively medium to high.",
